hi this is dr. Gregory Sadler I'm a  professor of philosophy and the  president and founder of an educational  consulting company called reason IO  where we put philosophy into practice  I've studied and taught philosophy for  over 20 years and I find that many  people run into difficulties reading  classic philosophical texts sometimes  it's the way things are said or how the  text is structured but the concepts  themselves are not always that  complicated and that's where I come in  to help students and lifelong learners  I've been producing longer lecture  videos and posting them to youtube many  viewers say they find them useful what  you're currently watching is part of a  new series of shorter videos each of  them focused on one core concept from an  important philosophical text I hope you  find it useful as well  in the course of his Ponce's these  unfinished work Paschal gives us a  really effective argumentative or  rhetorical strategy for telling people  they're wrong in such a way that they  can actually hear us in the French and  in the English translation it's  expressed in terms of correcting others  he says when we wish to correct with  advantage when we want to correct in  such a way that something good is going  to come out of it then we should follow  this strategy now many people may balk  at hearing this term correction because  they have negative associations with it  I you know don't correct me that that's  being mean to me in some way or  patronizing or disrespectful but it  doesn't really have that sense and if  you think about correction in general  what does it mean it means to set things  right and so for correcting a person's  mindset or their information or their  views on things we're hopefully not just  trying to be a jerk to them but we're  actually directing them towards what is  true something away from what they think  is true which may be false and towards  what is actually true and true not just  in a speculative or theoretical sense or  you know banal and factual sense but but  practical in terms of how they ought to  be regulating their life what they ought  to be worrying themselves to how to  prioritize all of those things are  matters in which we and other people do  in fact often require correction so the  fundamental question here is how best to  get someone to see where they've got  something wrong and paschal suggest that  we don't begin with saying Oh buddy  you've got things wrong we don't begin  by simply saying you're off base you're  mistaken  instead we  do something else before we even open  our mouths he says we have to notice  what side Cote in French meaning like a  bank or a side of something you know for  example you are seeing this chalk from  the opposite side that I am not really a  lot of change in the chalk right but  you're seeing a blue book I'm seeing  white pages now you're seeing white  pages I'm seeing a blue binder those are  different sides of things and when it  comes to all sorts of phenomena that we  might take positions on and be right or  wrong about there are many sides you  know a relationship for example you know  I think the relationship is  fundamentally of this sort but the other  person who is in that same relationship  thinks it's a different kind of  relationship when we get into conflicts  I think I was in the right and you drew  first blood with a terrible thing you  said and you think precisely the same  but change the people around on your  side so there can be very different  sides of different things we need to  notice what side the other person is on  rather than just simply pushing our own  point of view we need to extend them a  certain kind of charity or  open-mindedness or however you want to  put it the capacity to put ourselves in  somebody else's place and see things not  necessarily physically through their  eyes but at least try to see things the  way that they might see them we might  not be successful in this but if we  don't make the effort we're definitely  not going to be successful he also says  that if we do this we're going to notice  that there is some sort of truth from  that point of view from that side they  may not be completely correct as a  matter of fact they may be off base  about many things it could be that the  truth that they have on their side is  what we might call  an imaginary truth if we imagine that  things are like this or a hypothetical  truth if we imagine that all these  things are as the person perceives them  then it would be true that that you know  this is the case as the person does  believe we have to be able to grasp  whatever measure of truth there is on  their side why because that allows us to  connect with them that allows us to then  admit that truth to that person so it's  not just about being empathetic it's not  just about seeing their point of view  Pascal says you have to actually say to  that person okay I get you in that  respect I think that you actually do  have some things right here's the things  where I agree with you here's the things  where maybe I don't agree with you but I  can at least see how it appears that way  to you here's the things I need to think  about more because maybe you actually  are better informed about this than I am  and if you do this you're treating the  other person as if they have some sort  of seat at the table as if they are on  some level your epistemological equal as  if they get to have a real conversation  with you as a partner once you've done  that  Pascal says then you can also start  bringing up other sides to it and you  can start bringing up the other sides  from which what they're saying is  actually false you could you could do  this in multiple ways you can say look  you're saying this is always the case  but if you look at it from this point of  view there are exceptions to what you're  saying so what would you be arguing in  that case you'd be saying you're making  a universal rule it admits of a lot of  exceptions so it's not really a  universal rule it looks that way to you  because you haven't seen these  exceptions over here or you might say I  get what you're saying I understand why  things seem or feel  way that they do to you from the point  of view that you're coming at it but if  we look at it from this point of view  the actions that you think are noble are  actually self-centered or are malicious  or if you look at it from this point of  view the argument that you think I  started maybe began way back here with  something that you said and if you've  already recognized where the other  person has things right where they they  have a case that that's plausible and  you've acknowledged it then they'll be  more willing to hear you say where  they've got it wrong not necessarily  completely some people are just jerks  and won't listen to anything that you  say and if you make any admission that  they have some things right they will  take that as being completely right but  Pascal is talking about most people how  you can deal with other people  reasonably so you reveal the other sides  from which they've got things wrong  they've left things out they've not  taken something into consideration that  needs to be talked about what's the  effect of this here's where it gets very  interesting Paschal makes a distinction  here she says that the person realizes  that he or she is not mistaken the  French word for that is Pompeii  you know deceived they are not deceived  they're not a dummy they're not off base  entirely they're not just an idiot but  that they they didn't notice all the  different sides so there's a distinction  here between being like totally wrong  totally off base just a you know a fool  and missing something in the situation  which then you could take account of and  somehow incorporate into what you do in  fact have right or perhaps used to  modify what you've got right there is a  big difference between these if you tell  somebody that they're completely wrong  where do they have to start from they  can either accept your point  of you in a you know presented in a  rather despotic or tyrannical way a very  controlling way at the very least even  if it's done in a sort of you know this  is for the best  I just want to give you the correct view  on things you know if you're in a very  patronizing way they're they're stuck  either they accept it or they're an  idiot but if you're working with it in  the other way that paschal is suggesting  and you're saying listen I want you to  keep on believing these things over here  you're right about that but these things  over here you also need to take account  of them and and maybe if you bring them  all together you'll compromise on a few  of the things that you're committed to  right now that's a very different point  of view that's actually much more likely  to be successful because again you're  treating the person as an equal in some  sense as somebody who you value somebody  who has thoughts of his or her own so  the upshot of this is that the person  won't be offended he says they won't  take offense at missing some side of it  why not  because we all miss something right so  we need other people to help fill us in  about something that's why we read books  in the first place because we're not  self-sufficient when it comes to  understanding things but he does say  nobody likes being mistaken again the  French word Pompey decieved having  gotten things entirely wrong he says  perhaps that arises from the fact that  human beings can't naturally see  everything and that they naturally can't  err on the side that they look at but  they will err in other sides he says  something else that that contributes to  this picture a little bit later he tells  us people are generally better persuaded  by the reasons which they have  themselves discovered than by those  which have come into  minds of others so how can that process  of discovery take place that doesn't  mean that they have to like totally  figure it out on their own but they have  to be allowed the opportunity to see  what they missed out on and to you might  say take ownership of it and then try to  incorporate it into the rest of the  ideas or thoughts or propositions or  however you want to put it that they are  working with so if you adopt this sort  of strategy it's not gonna work every  single time and it shouldn't be done in  a purely cynical way this is not meant  to be sort of the sandwich if you're not  familiar with that rhetorical device  that's when you give some negative  feedback is as the meat in between two  bits of positive feedback you know  you're really doing a great job talking  with clients now I wanted to talk to you  about the fact that you need to shower a  lot more everybody is complaining how  much you stink at work but on the other  hand your spreadsheets are amazing  that's kind of artificial that's kind of  cynical right Paschal is not suggesting  you do anything like that instead he's  he's suggesting that you adopt this as a  kind of attitude towards other people if  you do this he doesn't mention this here  but if you do this  and you approach other people in this  way it's also going to have a positive  effect on your own point of view as you  start to realize the sides that you  yourself are leaving out when you are  staking claims when you are expressing  your point of view when you are telling  other people that this is the way things  are and that can be a good thing all  around so this is a very useful strategy  contained in Pascal's Ponce's and you  might want to give it a try  you
 

